begin;

select public.seed_cipmn_mock_module('CIPMN-MOD-006','[["Planning before scheduling","The team starts assigning dates before confirming objectives, scope, deliverables, assumptions, constraints or success criteria.","Clarify the project basis and approved scope before developing the detailed schedule.","Continue dating activities because scheduling will reveal the scope automatically.","Use the sponsor''s desired completion date as the only planning input.","Create a Gantt chart first and define objectives after execution begins.","A credible schedule is derived from clear objectives, scope, deliverables, assumptions, constraints and resource information."],["Defining project scope for the schedule","The schedule includes construction activities but omits approvals, training, handover and documentation required for acceptance.","Confirm the complete scope and include all deliverable and project-management work needed for acceptance.","Schedule only physical work because administrative activities do not consume time.","Add omitted work only if it causes a delay.","Treat acceptance activities as operational work outside the project.","The schedule should represent the full authorised scope, including enabling, governance, transition and acceptance work."],["WBS and the 100 percent rule","Work packages overlap and some deliverables appear in more than one branch of the WBS, creating double counting.","Restructure the WBS so it is mutually clear, deliverable-oriented and collectively represents 100 percent of the scope without duplication.","Keep duplicate work packages so every department feels represented.","Convert the WBS into a list of staff names.","Remove the WBS and schedule directly from invoices.","The 100 percent rule requires complete scope coverage while avoiding duplication across WBS elements."],["WBS dictionary and work-package definition","A work package is named ''system integration'' but no boundaries, deliverables, assumptions, acceptance criteria or responsible owner are defined.","Complete the WBS dictionary and define the work package sufficiently for estimating, assignment and control.","Leave the name broad so the team can decide the work during execution.","Use the project charter as the only work-package description.","Estimate duration before clarifying what completion means.","A WBS dictionary clarifies work-package content, boundaries, responsibility, assumptions and acceptance information."],["Activity definition from work packages","A work package is placed directly on the schedule as one six-month bar, making progress and dependencies impossible to manage.","Decompose the work package into manageable activities with clear outputs and completion conditions.","Keep one bar because fewer activities always produce better control.","Split the work only by calendar month, regardless of the work performed.","Create activities that describe departments rather than actions.","Activities should be detailed enough to estimate, sequence, assign, monitor and verify without creating unnecessary micro-management."],["Activity dependencies","Testing is scheduled to finish before the prototype it must test is available, because no logical relationship was recorded.","Define the appropriate dependency, such as finish-to-start, and validate the full network logic.","Keep the dates and ask testers to begin without the prototype.","Add more float to testing without correcting the dependency.","Use a cost code instead of a logical relationship.","Activity sequencing must reflect real logical dependencies; incorrect relationships produce infeasible schedules."],["Leads and lags","The team wants painting to begin two days after plastering starts, even though sections can overlap safely after curing conditions are met.","Model the justified overlap or waiting period explicitly with an appropriate relationship, lead or lag and document the assumption.","Change dates manually without recording the relationship.","Use negative duration for plastering.","Remove the dependency entirely because some overlap is possible.","Leads and lags should represent real timing conditions and be documented rather than hidden in arbitrary dates."],["Duration estimation and three-point estimates","A novel activity has significant uncertainty, but the planner records the most optimistic duration as a firm commitment.","Use expert judgement and a range-based method such as three-point estimating, documenting assumptions and risk.","Use the optimistic estimate because shorter plans drive better performance.","Double the optimistic estimate without analysing the work.","Set duration equal to the remaining calendar time.","Uncertain work should be estimated using evidence, assumptions and ranges rather than unsupported single-point optimism."],["Forward and backward pass","Management asks for early and late dates and total float, but the schedule contains only manually entered start and finish dates.","Build a logically linked network and perform forward and backward pass calculations.","Estimate float from the colour of Gantt bars.","Subtract actual cost from budget to obtain float.","Assume every activity has zero float.","Forward and backward passes calculate early dates, late dates and float from network logic and durations."],["Critical path and schedule optimisation","The project is late, and the team proposes shortening an activity with ten days of float while ignoring a delayed critical activity.","Focus optimisation on the current critical path and reassess criticality after each change.","Shorten the activity with the most float because it offers the largest saving.","Reduce every activity duration by the same percentage.","Change the baseline completion date instead of improving the plan.","Only changes affecting the critical path can directly shorten current project duration; critical paths may shift after optimisation."],["Crashing and fast tracking","The sponsor requests schedule recovery and the team is considering additional resources and overlapping sequential work.","Evaluate crashing and fast tracking for critical activities, including cost, feasibility, quality, safety and rework risk.","Overlap all activities regardless of technical dependency.","Add resources to every task without considering diminishing returns.","Use schedule compression without approval because the deadline is urgent.","Crashing trades cost for time; fast tracking increases overlap and risk. Both require targeted analysis and control."],["Resource allocation and levelling","The baseline assumes one engineer can perform three simultaneous full-time activities.","Resolve the over-allocation through resource levelling, reallocation, sequencing or approved capacity changes and recalculate the schedule.","Keep the plan because the engineer can prioritise informally.","Change the engineer''s utilisation display to 33 percent without changing work.","Remove resource data from the schedule.","A schedule must be resource-feasible; resolving over-allocation may change activity dates and the critical path."],["Schedule risk and contingency","A schedule uses single-point durations and contains no allowance for weather, approval or supply uncertainty.","Identify schedule risks, model uncertainty where appropriate and establish transparent contingency or buffers linked to risk exposure.","Hide extra time inside every activity so stakeholders cannot remove it.","Ignore uncertainty because contingency encourages delay.","Add the same arbitrary percentage to all activities.","Schedule contingency should be risk-based, visible and governed rather than hidden or arbitrary."],["Agile planning and scheduling","A product team tries to create a fixed detailed schedule for all features a year in advance despite expected learning and reprioritisation.","Use rolling-wave Agile planning with a prioritised product backlog, near-term Sprint planning and transparent progress measures.","Freeze every user story and estimate before the first Sprint.","Remove release goals and allow the team to work on any item.","Use a burndown chart as a substitute for backlog prioritisation.","Agile planning is adaptive and layered, with detailed near-term planning and progressive refinement of future work."],["Schedule baseline, monitoring and forecasting","Actual dates are entered, but no baseline comparison, variance trend, schedule performance indicator or completion forecast is produced.","Compare current performance with the approved baseline, analyse variance and trends, forecast completion and initiate corrective or change action.","Replace the baseline with actual dates each reporting period.","Report only the percentage of activities started.","Wait until the planned completion date before forecasting delay.","Schedule control requires a stable baseline, reliable progress data, variance analysis, forecasting and timely management action."]]'::jsonb);

commit;
